m, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: anemia, constipation, frequent urination, hyperviscosity syndrome, infections\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, bleeding, cellular immunodeficiency\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Acute Lymphoblastic Lymphoma/Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, blurred vision, cellular immunodeficiency\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Primary Hyperparathyroidism:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal circulating aldosterone, abnormal tongue morphology, abnormality of pancreas physiology\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Secondary Hyperparathyroidism:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, bone deformities, constipation, depression\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Tertiary Hyperparathyroidism:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, autosomal dominant inheritance, constipation, depression, frequent urination\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Vitamin D Toxicity:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, decreased circulating antibody level, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: aching muscles, binge eating, depression, digestive problems, dizziness\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Polycythemia Vera:\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, abnormal erythrocyte morphology, bleeding gums, burning sensation in extremities, confusion\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Multiple Myeloma:\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: anemia, constipation, frequent urination, hyperviscosity syndrome, infections\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: abdominal pain or fullness, anemia, autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, bleeding, cellular immunodeficiency\n\nChronic Myeloid Leukemia (CML) vs Acute Lymphoblastic Lymphoma/Leukemia:\n  Favours first:  abdominal swelling, abnormal basophil morphology, abnormal granulocyte morphology, abnormality of blood and blood-forming tissues, bleeding easily\n  Favours second: autosomal dominant somatic cell mutation, balance problems, bleeding from the gums, cellular immunodeficiency, chronic lymphatic leukemia"}
